!deck: Math Identities
!tags: Math Calculus Identity Limit
!type: Basic Math

!id: vjtstp5RFK
!front: \[ e = \lim_{n \to \infty} \ldots \]
!back: \[ e = \lim_{n \to \infty} \left( 1 + \frac{1}{n} \right)^n \]
!extra:
For positive integers \( n \), take logarithms to turn the power into a product:
\[
\begin{align*}
    L_n
    &= \ln\left( \left(1 + \frac{1}{n}\right)^n \right) \\
    &= n \ln\left(1 + \frac{1}{n}\right) \\
    &= \frac{\ln(1 + 1/n) - \ln 1}{1/n}.
\end{align*}
\]
The fraction is the difference quotient for \( \ln \) at \( 1 \), with step \( 1/n \to 0 \). Hence
\[ L_n \to \left.\frac{d}{dx}\ln x\right|_{x=1} = 1. \]
By continuity of the exponential function, exponentiating gives
\[ \left(1 + \frac{1}{n}\right)^n = \exp\left( L_n \right) \to e^1 = e. \]
~~~

!id: 5tnHPoehhw
!front: \[ e^x = \lim_{n \to \infty} \ldots \]
!back: \[ e^x = \lim_{n \to \infty} \left( 1 + \frac{x}{n} \right)^n \]
!extra:
Fix \( x \in \mathbb{R} \). For \( x = 0 \), both sides equal \( 1 \). Otherwise, for sufficiently large positive integers \( n \), we have \( 1 + x/n > 0 \), so take logarithms to turn the power into a product:
\[
\begin{align*}
    L_n
    &= \ln\left( \left(1 + \frac{x}{n}\right)^n \right) \\
    &= n \ln\left(1 + \frac{x}{n}\right) \\
    &= x \cdot \frac{\ln(1 + x/n) - \ln 1}{x/n}.
\end{align*}
\]
The fraction is the difference quotient for \( \ln \) at \( 1 \), with step \( x/n \to 0 \). Hence
\[ L_n \to x \cdot \left.\frac{d}{dt}\ln t\right|_{t=1} = x. \]
By continuity of the exponential function, exponentiating gives
\[ \left(1 + \frac{x}{n}\right)^n = \exp\left( L_n \right) \to e^x. \]
~~~

!id: co0b8kRcJm
!front: \[ e = \lim_{h \to 0} \ldots \]
!back: \[ e = \lim_{h \to 0} \left( 1 + h \right)^{1 / h} \]
!extra:
For \( 0 < |h| < 1 \), we have \( 1+h > 0 \), so take logarithms to turn the power into a product:
\[
\begin{align*}
    L_h
    &= \ln\left( (1+h)^{1/h} \right) \\
    &= \frac{1}{h}\ln(1+h) \\
    &= \frac{\ln(1+h) - \ln 1}{h}.
\end{align*}
\]
The fraction is the difference quotient for \( \ln \) at \( 1 \), with step \( h \to 0 \). Hence
\[ L_h \to \left.\frac{d}{dx}\ln x\right|_{x=1} = 1. \]
By continuity of the exponential function, exponentiating gives
\[ (1+h)^{1/h} = \exp\left( L_h \right) \to e^1 = e. \]
~~~

!id: RsC1HP4Yhn
!front: \[ e^x = \lim_{h \to 0} \ldots \]
!back: \[ e^x = \lim_{h \to 0} \left( 1 + h \right)^{x / h} \]
!extra:
Fix \( x \in \mathbb{R} \). For \( 0 < |h| < 1 \), we have \( 1+h > 0 \), so take logarithms to turn the power into a product:
\[
\begin{align*}
    L_h
    &= \ln\left( (1+h)^{x/h} \right) \\
    &= \frac{x}{h}\ln(1+h) \\
    &= x \cdot \frac{\ln(1+h) - \ln 1}{h}.
\end{align*}
\]
The fraction is the difference quotient for \( \ln \) at \( 1 \), with step \( h \to 0 \). Hence
\[ L_h \to x \cdot \left.\frac{d}{dt}\ln t\right|_{t=1} = x. \]
By continuity of the exponential function, exponentiating gives
\[ (1+h)^{x/h} = \exp\left( L_h \right) \to e^x. \]
~~~

!id: zVX3ZAqxkr
!front: \[ \ln a = \lim_{x \to \ldots} \ldots \]
!back: \[ \ln a = \lim_{x \to 0} \frac{a^x - 1}{x} \]
!extra:
Fix \( a > 0 \). For \( a = 1 \), the quotient is \( 0 \) for every \( x \ne 0 \), so the limit equals \( \ln 1 = 0 \). Otherwise, write the power in exponential form:
\[
\begin{align*}
    Q_x
    &= \frac{a^x - 1}{x} \\
    &= \frac{e^{x\ln a} - 1}{x} \\
    &= \ln a \cdot \frac{e^{x\ln a} - e^0}{x\ln a}.
\end{align*}
\]
The fraction is the difference quotient for \( \exp \) at \( 0 \), with step \( x\ln a \to 0 \). Hence
\[ Q_x \to \ln a \cdot \left.\frac{d}{dt}e^t\right|_{t=0} = \ln a \cdot 1 = \ln a. \]

---

The special case \( a = e \) gives \( \lim_{x \to 0} \dfrac{e^x - 1}{x} = 1 \).
~~~

!id: eD2Z0zWOJ6
!front: \[ \lim_{n \to \infty} n^{1/n} = \ldots \]
!back: \[ \lim_{n \to \infty} n^{1/n} = 1 \]
!extra:
Write \( n^{1/n} = e^{\ln n / n} \). By L'Hôpital's rule applied to \( \ln x / x \) as \( x \to \infty \),
\[ \lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{\ln x}{x} = \lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{1/x}{1} = 0, \]
and since \( \exp \) is continuous,
\[ n^{1/n} = e^{\ln n / n} \to e^0 = 1. \]
~~~

!id: HFOtuFozwk
!front: \[ \lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{x^n}{e^x} = \ldots \]
!back: \[ \lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{x^n}{e^x} = 0 \]
~~~

!id: C7fRXFwhSJ
!front: \[ \lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{\ln x}{x^a} = \ldots, \quad a > 0 \]
!back: \[ \lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{\ln x}{x^a} = 0, \quad a > 0 \]
~~~

!id: ZptgdpueDf
!front: \[ \lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{x^n}{n!} = \ldots \]
!back: \[ \lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{x^n}{n!} = 0 \]
~~~
